{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 28, :comments ["<b>In the way of righteousness is life, and in the pathway thereof there is no death.</b> (Proverbs 12:28)" "<b>King Shelomo</b>, upon him be peace, comes to declare in this verse (Proverbs 12) the praise of the trait of righteousness (צדקה), for by guarding it there is great reward, and the days of one who sustains it are increased. And behold, it is the salvation of both soul and body, and it prevails over the decree of the arrangement of the stars, for it is well known that the days of a person are decreed according to the rule of the constellation, and this is attested by what is written (1 Samuel 26): ״או יומו יבא״ — that is, his time will draw near, and his final day. And likewise (1 Kings 2): ״ויקרבו ימי דוד למות״. (Deuteronomy 31): ״הן קרבו ימיך למות״. And the sense of ״drawing near״ is only concerning the appointed time that is decreed. And even though the arrangement of the stars necessitates that a person's days be decreed, there is a power above the force of the arrangement, according to the measure of reward and punishment, to add to what is decreed — as He added to Ḥizkiyahu, as it is said (Isaiah 38): ״הנני יוסיף על ימיך ט״ו שנה״ — fifteen years; and to shorten from what is decreed — as He shortened from Yehoram son of Aḥazyahu, in punishment for his having inquired of an idol in his illness, for thus the prophet said to him (2 Kings 1): ״כה אמר ה׳ יען אשר שלחת מלאכים לדרוש בבעל זבוב אלהי עקרון המבלי אין אלהים בישראל לדרוש בדברו לכן המטה אשר עלית שם לא תרד ממנה כי מות תמות״. Therefore Shelomo said that צדקה is an exalted trait, its power is mighty, and its fruit is life — this is what he said: ״בארח צדקה חיים״, that is to say, added life beyond what is decreed. And as our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded on Binyamin the righteous, who was appointed over the charity fund: a woman came to him in a year of famine and said to him, 'Rabbi, sustain me.' He said to her, 'I swear, there is nothing in the charity fund.' She said to him, 'If you do not sustain me, behold a woman and her seven sons are dying.' He arose and sustained her from his own. After some days Binyamin the righteous fell ill and was dying. The ministering angels said before the Holy One, blessed be He: 'Master of the Universe, You said: whoever sustains one soul from Israel is as if he sustained a whole world, and Binyamin the righteous, who sustained a woman and her seven sons — should he die in but a few years?' It was resolved: add for him twenty-two years. ״ודרך נתיבה אל מות״ — that is to say: and since this is so, it is certain that in the path of the way he will not find death, for whoever treads the pathway will not see death before what is decreed, and before his time he will not die. And as it is written (Proverbs 10): ״וצדקה תציל ממות״, that is, it shields him so that he not die before his time, for since צדקה adds to what is decreed, all the more so does it bring the days to their full decreed measure, and he will not encounter death before his time arrives. The essence of צדקה is that a person gives of his own to one who needs. And behold, there are many levels: there is צדקה where a person gives a small coin to a poor person of the nations. Greater than this is giving to a poor person of Israel from another city. Higher than this is giving to the poor of his own city, as it is said (Exodus 22): ״את העני עמך״ — the poor person who is with you. Higher than this is giving to a poor person who is his relative, as it is said (Isaiah 58): ״ומבשרך לא תתעלם״. Higher than this is sustaining his children. And thus our Rabbis of blessed memory said: What is written (Psalms 106): ״אשרי שמרי משפט עושה צדקה בכל עת״ — and is it possible for a person to do צדקה at all times? Rather, this is one who feeds his sons and daughters when they are small. Higher than this is one who sustains his father and his mother, for he fulfills the commandment of honoring father and mother, and it is a great צדקה than which there is none greater; therefore its reward is length of days, as it is written (Exodus 20): ״למען יאריכון ימיך״. This is ״בארח צדקה חיים״. And behold, this is measure for measure. And thus we find regarding Yosef, that he sustained his father seventeen years, and it is measure for measure: in the merit of the צדקה that Yaakov did with him by sustaining him seventeen years — for when Yosef parted from his father he was seventeen years old, as it is said (Genesis 37): ״אלה תולדות יעקב יוסף בן שבע עשרה שנה״ — and in the merit that Yaakov sustained Yosef for seventeen years, we find that Yosef sustained Yaakov in Egypt seventeen years, measure for measure. And this is what is written: ״ויחי יעקב בארץ מצרים שבע עשרה שנה״." "<b>And the days of Yaakov, the years of his life, were seven years and a hundred and forty years.</b> This parashah is closed (סתומה) for two reasons. The first: because now the exile was beginning, and their hearts were closed off from the distress of the servitude. The second: because Yaakov sought to reveal the End (הקץ) to his sons, and it was closed off (נסתם) from him — for what is written, ״את אשר יקרא אתכם באחרית הימים״, the intent is the days of the Messiah at the end of this long exile in which we stand today, for all the affairs and experiences of Yaakov are an allusion to this third exile of ours, for Yaakov was the third of the fathers. And since the matter of the Messiah is alluded to in the totality of his words, as he said, ״עד כי יבא שילה״, this parashah came closed to allude to the sealed End that he wished to reveal and was closed off from him. And thus Daniel said (Daniel 12:4): ״כי סתומים וחתומים הדברים עד עת קץ ישוטטו רבים ותרבה הדעת״. And thus Yeshayahu said, upon him be peace (Isaiah 9:6): ״לםרבה המשרה ולשלום אין קץ״, and the mem comes closed, unlike the rule, for normally a mem in the middle of a word is open (פתוחה), and this one is closed (סתומה), to allude that the greatness and dominion of Israel — behold, it is closed in the time of exile. And we find in Ezra an open mem at the end of a word, and this too is not according to the rule, and it is what is written (Nehemiah 2:3–4): ״בחומות ירושלים אשר המ פרוצים ושעריה אכלו באש״. And they said in the Midrash that the allusion comes: when the walls of Jerusalem, which are now breached and broken in the time of exile, become closed — then the dominion that is closed will be opened. And just as these letters came unlike the rule of the other letters and carry an allusion in them, so too this parashah is closed unlike the rule of the other parashiyyot, to allude to what we have said. And thus they said in the Aggadah: Yaakov our father saw that all the letters were present in the names of the twelve tribes, his sons, except for the letters ח and ט. Said Yaakov: since there is no חטא (sin) in them, they are worthy of having the End revealed to them. But when he saw that there was no קו״ף and צד״י in them, he said: they are not worthy of having the End revealed to them. Therefore he closed it and did not reveal it, and therefore this parashah is closed (סתומה)." "<b>Seventeen years.</b> From what was stated earlier, where Yaakov said to Pharaoh, ״ימי שני מגורי שלשים ומאת שנה״, and here the verse specifies all the days of his life, which were 147 years, we could have deduced that in Egypt he stood seventeen years; and why was it necessary to write, ״ויחי יעקב בארץ מצרים שבע עשרה שנה״? But the entire Torah is allusions; there is no word or letter in it that was not said for an essential and necessary purpose. And it comes to allude to measure for measure (מדה כנגד מדה), for the reason I mentioned in the proem — and this is the Midrash of our Rabbis of blessed memory."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 47, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:21:23.642200267Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}